{"type": "thinking", "text": "Good: \"NaN\" rejected. The parser is robust.\n\nTest \"Infinity\"? Likely rejected too. Let's try \"1e3\" maybe accepted (scientific notation) — probably not expected for weight but not harmful. Test \"  70 \" with spaces. Hmm, type_text can include spaces; numeric keyboard has Space. If parser trims, okay.\n\nActually, let me think about what likely bug was introduced by \"single number parser for every input\". Common bug: the parser handles decimal comma but also treats '.' as decimal; but maybe for \"70,5\" it's fine. Another: thousands separators \"1,234\" parsed as 1.234 instead of 1234 (or vice versa). In body weight, \"1,234\" would be weird. In lab values, \"1,234\" could be a legit thousands number? Not likely.\n\nMaybe the bug is inconsistent handling of trailing separators or \"-\". Let me test \"70.\" (trailing dot) and \",5\" (leading comma). These are edge cases that a parser may accept as 70 / 0.5. Not necessarily bug.\n\nHmm, maybe I should look more carefully at the lab result editor since it has multiple inputs (Estradiol, Testosterone, and custom). The unified parser could produce inconsistency between fields of different units, e.g., converting units. Wait — there's unit conversion in that editor: selecting ng/dL vs pg/mL converts the entered value? Let's test: enter 100 pg/mL, switch to pmol/L → should convert to 367.1 pmol/L. If conversion uses different parser, might break.\n\nActually the phrase \"Settings measurement editor\" — maybe it's specifically the sheet that appears when you tap a unit chip? Hmm.\n\nLet me step back and consider the \"Settings\" area is broad; the changed item only one specific. Maybe the intended bug is in Body weight editor when value uses comma: entering \"70,5\" saved as 70.5 correctly. Fine.\n\nWhat about entering a value with a trailing comma \"70,\"? The parser might accept it as 70 which is fine.\n\nLet's test \"Infinity\" quickly and then move on. Type over NaN.\n\nActually there's a subtle possibility: the shared parser might accept a value like \"0x10\" or \"1_000\". Eh.\n\nLet me test \"1e3\" — if accepted as 1000 kg, that's odd but not clearly wrong.\n\nI think the more suspect area is the medication group schedule summary and journal pagination. Let me allocate time there.\n\nBut first restore weight to 70.5 to avoid weird state. Type \"70.5\" and Save.\n\nNeed to clear \"NaN\" select all etc. Use Clear? That clears the saved value with confirmation; not needed. Let me select all & type. Ugh. Alternative: just tap Save with NaN shows error. Use long-press select all → Cut → type.\n\nActually the field is focused. I can press DEL 3 times then type \"70.5\"."}
