is recomputed to the first dose time of that new day's schedule? But first dose is 9:00 AM. Unless with night owl and 3:00 AM dose, the \"day\" starts at 4:00? no.\n\nHold on. Maybe I accidentally changed the time when interacting with the date picker? The date picker overlay was full-screen; the time row was behind. My taps at (792,1326) etc. were date cells. No.\n\nHmm, let me check if the start time resets to 4:00 AM each time the date picker is used. It went to 4:00 after Nov 20 and stayed 4:00 after Nov 21. If it's reset on each date change, it would have set 4:00 both times (same). Could be a fixed default \"4:00 AM\" reset.\n\nLet me test toggling night owl off and then changing date again; if start time becomes 12:00 AM, that supports \"start of logical day\" reset.\n\nActually, I recall the group editor earlier when we first opened (after creation) showed START TIME \"6:36 AM\". And in Add group, START TIME \"Now\". So the stored start time was 6:36 AM (creation). After changing date to Nov 20, it's 4:00 AM. So date change reset time to 4:00 AM. Why 4:00 AM? Night owl cutoff is 4:00 AM. Maybe that's the \"logical day start\". Interesting.\n\nThis is likely an injected bug? Or an existing quirk. The area asked: \"editing medication groups\". We should report if it's a clear defect. Changing start date unexpectedly overwrites start time — user-visible and lossy. I can report it, pointing at the START TIME row showing \"4:00 AM\" while previously \"6:36 AM\". But the developer may reject if expected (e.g., start time is derived from \"Now\" and changing date resets to beginning of day). Hmm.\n\nLet me gather more evidence: cancel this edit (to discard), reopen, note start time, change date only, see if start time changes. Cancel now."}
